 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 7,
   "plaque": "shropshire_flax_mill",
   "title": "Ditherington Flax Mill",
   "text": "Built in 1797 to spin flax into linen thread, with a frame of cast-iron columns and beams instead of timber, so it would not burn down like other mills. It was the first iron-framed building in the world, the grandfather of every skyscraper. Later it malted barley for brewers, and it has been restored."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 8,
   "plaque": "shropshire_castle",
   "title": "Shrewsbury Castle",
   "text": "Roger de Montgomery built a castle here in red sandstone soon after 1066, to guard the only dry way into the town, across the neck of the river loop. Thomas Telford tidied it up around 1790 and added Laura's Tower as a summerhouse. It now holds the Soldiers of Shropshire Museum."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "shropshire_station",
   "title": "Shrewsbury Station",
   "text": "Opened in 1848 and built in a Tudor Gothic style to suit the old town. Part of the station stands on a bridge over the Severn, which makes it one of very few stations in Britain built over a river."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "shropshire_darwin_statue",
   "title": "Darwin's Statue",
   "text": "Charles Darwin was born in Shrewsbury on 12 February 1809 and went to school in this very building, which was Shrewsbury School then and is the town library now. He was a poor pupil by his own account and spent his free time collecting beetles. His statue has sat here since 1897."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "shropshire_magpie",
   "title": "Black and White Town",
   "text": "Shrewsbury has more than six hundred listed buildings, and many are timber-framed in the Shropshire black-and-white style, nicknamed magpie work: oak beams tarred black, panels whitewashed. The upper floors jut out over the street, and folk say it was because upstairs space was taxed less."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "shropshire_shuts",
   "title": "The Shuts",
   "text": "The narrow alleys between Shrewsbury's streets are called shuts, because they could be shut off at night with a gate. Grope Lane, Gullet Passage and Peacock Passage are still there. Grope Lane's name is exactly as rude as it sounds, and it is centuries old."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "shropshire_wyle_cop",
   "title": "Wyle Cop",
   "text": "The steep street down to the English Bridge. Henry Tudor is said to have stayed in a house on Wyle Cop in 1485, on his way to win the crown at Bosworth. The house is still there, with its timbers leaning companionably on the neighbours."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "shropshire_quarry",
   "title": "The Quarry and the Dingle",
   "text": "Twenty-nine acres of park inside the river loop, laid out in 1719 where stone was once dug. The old quarry pit became the Dingle, a sunken flower garden that Percy Thrower, the television gardener, looked after as the town's parks superintendent from 1946 to 1974."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 43,
   "plaque": "shropshire_flower_show",
   "title": "Shrewsbury Flower Show",
   "text": "Held in the Quarry every August since 1875, which makes it one of the longest-running flower shows in the world. Marquees of prize dahlias, giant leeks, fireworks over the river, and a great deal of serious talk about compost."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "shropshire_st_chads",
   "title": "St Chad's Church",
   "text": "A round church, finished in 1792 after the old St Chad's fell down. Charles Darwin was baptised here, and the graveyard holds a stone for Ebenezer Scrooge, left over from filming A Christmas Carol in the town in 1984."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 48,
   "plaque": "shropshire_kingsland",
   "title": "Kingsland Bridge",
   "text": "An iron bridge of 1883, built by a private company and still a toll bridge, one of the very few left in Britain. Walkers cross free. The keeper will happily tell you it is cheaper than the Severn Bridge."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 56,
   "plaque": "shropshire_school",
   "title": "Shrewsbury School",
   "text": "The school Darwin went to, founded in 1552, moved up here to Kingsland in 1882, leaving its old building to the town library. Its rowers are out on the Severn below most mornings, being shouted at through a megaphone."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "shropshire_the_mount",
   "title": "The Mount",
   "text": "Charles Darwin was born in this house on 12 February 1809, the son of a doctor. He sailed on HMS Beagle from 1831 to 1836, and in 1859 published On the Origin of Species, the book that explained evolution by natural selection. It is still a private house, so wave nicely."
  },
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "shropshire_frankwell",
   "title": "Frankwell",
   "text": "The suburb across the Welsh Bridge was once outside the town walls, and a free market for Welsh traders who wanted to avoid the town's tolls. The Welsh Bridge itself dates from 1795."
  },
  {
   "x": 63,
   "y": 29,
   "plaque": "shropshire_abbey",
   "title": "Shrewsbury Abbey",
   "text": "Founded in 1083 by Roger de Montgomery as a Benedictine monastery. In 1138 the monks brought the bones of St Winefride here from Wales, a story the writer Ellis Peters turned into the first of her Brother Cadfael mysteries. Cadfael is made up; the abbey and its herb garden are not."
  },
  {
   "x": 66,
   "y": 36,
   "plaque": "shropshire_owen",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Wilfred Owen",
   "text": "The poet Wilfred Owen grew up in Shrewsbury. He was killed in France a week before the end of the First World War, and his mother got the telegram here on 11 November 1918, as the town's bells rang for the Armistice. A sculpture by the abbey remembers him."
  },
